#!/usr/bin/env bash

## Description: Add the Drupal test bootstrap to PhpStorm's PHPUnit settings.
## Usage: phpstorm-config
## Example: ddev phpstorm-config
## ExecRaw: true

set -e

# Canvas maps the project root 1:1 to /var/www/html, so PhpStorm's stock DDEV
# integration already configures a working interpreter and path mappings. The one
# thing it gets wrong for Drupal is the PHPUnit bootstrap: it runs PHPUnit with
# --no-configuration and only the composer autoloader, which does not register
# the Drupal\Tests\<module>\ PSR-4 test namespace. Tests whose parent class lives
# there (e.g. a *TestBase) then fail with "Class ... not found". This patches
# .idea/php.xml to add web/core/tests/bootstrap.php to the PHPUnit interpreter
# settings. @see .ddev/scripts/scaffold-phpstorm-config.php

# The helper reads/writes the host .idea via the project mount, so the project
# must be running.
if ! ddev exec true >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "The project is not running. Start it first with: ddev start"
  exit 1
fi

# Run the patcher from inside the web container. It writes /var/www/html/.idea,
# which is the host .idea/ (the Mutagen-synced project root).
ddev exec 'php /mnt/ddev_config/scripts/scaffold-phpstorm-config.php'

cat <<'EOF'

PhpStorm PHPUnit bootstrap configured.

If PhpStorm was open, close and reopen the project (or invalidate caches) so it
re-reads .idea/php.xml. Then click the gutter arrow next to any test method to
run it inside the ddev-canvas-web container.

If the command reported no interpreter to patch, open Settings -> PHP -> Test
Frameworks, add a PHPUnit-by-remote-interpreter config for the DDEV interpreter,
apply, then re-run `ddev phpstorm-config`.
EOF
